{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1014508-forgotten-foes-of-the-garou-the-gamor","id":1014508,"title":"Forgotten Foes of the Garou: The Gamor","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-12-08T16:28:10-08:00","id":1014508,"title":"Forgotten Foes of the Garou: The Gamor","content":"One thing I've noticed while reading some of the earliest sourcebooks are the concepts that didn't take hold and weren't developed later on. There are various monsters the books mention, but which never seem to show up again. One of these is the Gamor. I'll give a summary here, but what I'm interested in is hearing any ideas the forum has in developing the monster so that it can become something people would be interested in having in their chronicles.<br /> <br /> The Gamor is found in the 1993 sourcebook The Ways of the Wolf. It is not a Bane or Fomori, simply a monster. It is a &quot;demonic animal&quot; that can shapechange into one specific human form. Other than doing aggravated damage in its natural form, it doesn't have any other powers. Its stats are well within normal for a human. It has decent physical stats and fighting abilities, but really does not pose any physical threat. It might be a tough opponent for one Garou, but even many Cliaths should be able to defeat it in Crinos, and a pack of PCs will kill it quickly. The only other high stats are in Manipulation, Empathy, Subterfuge, and Stealth.<br /> <br /> Its descriptions says its feeds on mankind's pain and suffering. It seems to do so merely by being able to sense it with its Empathy, so it doesn't need to kill people, torture them, or otherwise directly harm them. The Gamor live among humanity in secret while doing everything they can to increase pain and suffering in society. They have achieved a &quot;vast infiltration of society.&quot; However, they only prey on humanity, so most of the Garou who do know about them, don't see them as any major threat. So they must not be interested in industrial pollution. Strangely, lone Gamor will move far away from everyone to remote mountain locations where they can hide their resources underground like when survivalists stockpile. So they expect some kind of great disaster. Very few creatures even know about the Gamor. Vampires and Mages don't, and even only a few Garou seem to know of them. Most of these are Silent Striders who travel the world looking for them. <br /> <br /> In terms of history, the Gamor seem to be ancient beings, perhaps bred from humans in the days before the Garou. A line says that in the days when North America was a hellhole on Gaian they were the Wyrm's greatest servants. However, when the Croatan, Uktena, and Wendigo came, they fell from favor. Presumably, then they originated in the Western hemisphere. Unknown when they managed to reach the Eastern hemisphere. Strangely, even though the Pure Ones must have encountered them early on, there is no indication they particularly remember them or care to investigate them now.<br /> <br /> That's it. With few powers, not being a physical threat, and a fairly sketchy background, it is not surprising players did not flock to this creature by including it in their games. This is a weak opponent which does not make a memorable foe. I don't know of any legendary creature called Gamor, so I think this was just made up for the book. Which is fine except it eliminates any other source material which could be inspiring. It is a strange inclusion for a book about the lupus breed. Presumably the idea that lupus PCs would want to raid and attack the hidden underground bunkers of the Gamor was the main intent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-08T16:33:01-08:00","id":1014510,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif\">Here are some of my additions. There are some interesting things about the Gamors First, their true form is of a &quot;demonic animal&quot;. It could fit into that role whenever an ST needed a monstrous animal foe. However, that is sandbagged when we learn it spends most of its time hidden among humanity in the city creating &quot;suffering&quot; and feeding on that simply by its Empathy. But at least it is something. Presumably in the earliest days of the Garou, the Gamor was a predator on Stone Age nomadic bands in its &quot;demon animal&quot; form.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif\">Second, its status as an ancient creature that existed before the Garou at a time when &quot;North America was a hellhole of corruption&quot; means it probably existed before humanity (even though the description also speculates it was bred from humans in the past). They were the greatest servants of the Wyrm in their time. This creates all kind of Lovecraftian goodness. The Gamor are the scions of a corrupt pre-human civilization. Since only a few Silent Striders seem to have uncovered them nowadays, it likely means the Gamor are located all over the world, and that a select few Striders have pieced a few things together. So after the Gamor civilization in North America collapse, they may have survived by somehow breeding with humans and scattering. Eventually they ensconced themselves in every civilization around the globe encouraging anything that would lead to suffering to people. So the Gamor have to have some kind of heritage and memory of their former power, and are probably somehow in contact with each other around the globe in order to spread corruption and suffering.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif\">Third, the Gamor must be expecting some kind of societal disaster as they are preparing stockpiles of resources in the remote wilderness. Presumably, they intend to wait things out once things go to crap. Perhaps they expect a time period when civilization falls to barbarians like during Rome, a nuclear winter or environmental disaster that collapses civilization, some kind of economic collapse that causes mass suffering but from which humanity as a whole will easily survive, or maybe they expect to be able to survive through the Apocalypse.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif\">Fourth, in terms of the Triatic Wyrm, they must be servants of the Defiler Wyrm. They like to cause poverty, hardship, and are behind many forms of abuse. I would not be surprised if they worked with groups like the Seventh Generation. They like to engineer disasters just to enjoy it - so they may plan &quot;parties&quot; where they can witness a train derailment that kills dozens, or fund terrorist organizations so they can blow up random buildings.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif\">Any plots involving them must involve multiple Gamor. They simply aren't dangerous by themselves to Garou. And since they don't have any mind control powers, they have to work through normal human society. They must be cooperating in some kind of conspiracy. They seem kind of like an anti-League of Assassins from Batman Begins. Instead of working to destroy a corrupt civilization in secret so that a new one can emerge, they are trying to cause civilization's downfall by corrupting it. So we have some kind of evil Illuminati conspiracy. I imagine their are posts on YouTube detailing the nature of this conspiracy in Hollywood, big business, and the British Royal family except that instead of being Reptilians, they are demonic shapechanging animals.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif\">That is kind of interesting, but they still seem to need something more. They can be the mastermind of some over the top evil, but they are still too weak as antagonists and not distinctive enough. I'm thinking we need more details on their pre-human civilization and map that to how they are recreating it in human society to spread pain and suffering. They also need to do things like try to uncover old artifacts of their North American civilization, collect whatever remains of their heritage, and to get some new additional powers as a result which can make them better combat opponents for the PCs.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-15T07:40:24-08:00","id":1017646,"title":"","content":"I have played around with the Gamor off and on for the last well longer than I like to recall. The Gamor do breed with humans that is in the write-up somewhere.<br /> <br /> \u200bWhile physically weak I augmented them with specialized rites that increased their power based on the devastation they created. <br /> Unfortunately I do not know where the notebook is with the rites and effects. I do know I paired them with the Vor'lag from the Apocalypse sourcebook that gave them the muscle needed to be a credible threat to a pack of Garou.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/293-lncbill","name":"lncbill","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar293_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-15T21:22:44-08:00","id":1017939,"title":"","content":"Someone actually used them! Amazing. I am still working on some ideas to expand on them and make them something I can find worthwhile to include in game. But this week I somehow got sidetracked with ideas concerning the Sons of Typhon which I may post on the forums later next week. Don't know when I'll return to this post, but I intend to. I'm stealing various ideas from Clive Barker's Hellbound Heart (but not the various Hellraiser movies) and Laird Barron's Children of Old Leech mythos.<br /> <br /> If you ever remember a vague summary of the rites you created, Incbill, I'd find it interesting. It sounds like you have some specific ideas on their use. If you can share any game experiences or intended uses, that would be great.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-15T22:25:25-08:00","id":1017966,"title":"","content":"To me it seems more interesting if they're NOT powerful or possessed of significant magic, but just very good at working behind the scenes and helping skew society toward the creation of misery. It doesn't sound as if they're actually concerned with the Garou at all - might be eye opening for the latter to realize there are things out there that don't consider them an important opponent toward their service to the Wyrm, and operate almost entirely outside their areas of knowledge.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-16T07:55:19-08:00","id":1018087,"title":"","content":"I don't work this weekend so I will look for the notebook that has my notes.<br /> \u200bI remember that in part the rites were inspired by an industrial accident. Living in Pa and having visited Centrailia I do know that I used these events to help focus the Gamor.<br /> \u200bAll of the rites were slow and aimed at causing emotional devastation over not a few people but entire communities,<br /> The most basic rite without notes went something like this:<br /> \u200bThe Heart of Despair this rite is only conducted when there is little chance of interference from the Garou or Corax nosing around. This rite is intended to supply enough misery to draw as many banes as possible to the area. The Gamor spends several days preparing the site where the rite will take place the Gamor has no control over what happens but if successful the tragedy caused will destroy lives. AN example of the successful use of this rite was the town of Centrailia Pennsylvania which went from a small town to an abandoned and later destroyed town over the course of 5 years.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/293-lncbill","name":"lncbill","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar293_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-16T17:24:47-08:00","id":1018271,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1014508-forgotten-foes-of-the-garou-the-gamor?p=1017966#post1017966\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It doesn't sound as if they're actually concerned with the Garou at all - might be eye opening for the latter to realize there are things out there that don't consider them an important opponent toward their service to the Wyrm, and operate almost entirely outside their areas of knowledge.</div> </div> </div> </div>I do like that aspect as well, but as written it is extremely hard to understand why they're included in a sourcebook for the game at all. I like the idea of &quot;Here are opponents we should be fighting since it is of the Wyrm, but everything they do are things we really don't care about&quot;, but not the idea of a group whose interactions and motivations are so limited that there's no knowledge or contact at all. &quot;No one knows about them except for a few Silent Striders,&quot; doesn't give me many hooks to understand how to use them in a game.<br /> <br /> I would like to have an interesting faction write up on them that at least make them sound interesting, and then understand how they'd be good opponents if a pack of PCs somehow encountered them anyway. I'm working on that, but still in the brainstorming stage.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-07T13:01:51-08:00","id":1547623,"title":"","content":"I'm resurrecting a very old thread of mine. I think I found the possible inspiration for the Gamor. I recently started reading the <a href=\"https://www.valancourtbooks.com/john-the-balladeer-1988.html\" target=\"_blank\">Silver John stories</a> of Manly Wade Wellman. I've been interested in them ever since I read <a href=\"https://hellboy.fandom.com/wiki/The_Crooked_Man_(comic_story)\" target=\"_blank\">Hellboy: the Crooked Man</a>*. It just took me a while to find a copy of the stories. I've since read other stories by Wellman (one reason is that Wellman is specifically mentioned as inspiration in Rage Across Appalachia). As part of that I've come across his villains the Shonokins who appear in his Silver John novel After Dark and many stories about his occult detective, John Thunstone.<br /> <br /> There are various similarities between the Shonokins and the Gamor. The Shonokins:<br /> <br /> are a sub-human race<br /> they can appear humans (albeit with some tell tale signs)<br /> know black magic<br /> ruled the Americas before the arrival of the American Indians<br /> lost their domain after the arrival of the Indians and subsequently went into hiding<br /> use magic and wealth to manipulate and control people<br /> they would like to regain control of their lost lands<br /> <br /> This tracks closely with the major points about the Gamor. There is not a one to one match, but they are very similar. The Shonokins do have several weaknesses (and other differences) that don't show up in the Gamor, but that makes sense because those weaknesses are there so that Wellman's heroes can defeat them while the needs of an RPG are different. If the Shonokins did not inspire the Gamor, then their similarities are quite a coincidence. <br /> <br /> The only thing that makes me question it is if the Gamor are stand-ins for the Shonokins, I am very surprised they were not mentioned in Rage Across Appalachia whose author was inspired by Wellman. There is only a two year gap between Ways of the Wolf (where the Gamor appeared) in 1993 and Rage Across Appalachia in 1995, and Bill Bridges was the developer for both.<br /> <br /> However, even if the Shonokins were not the inspiration for the Gamor, there's still a lot I can steal from Wellman's stories about how to portray them. If they are the inspiration of the Shonokins, I wonder if the reference to lone Silent Striders opposing them is a reference to Wellman's characters Silver John and Thunstone.<br /> <br /> For those interested, you can read a lot of the stories that mention the Shonokins in the free (public domain) pdf <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/WellmanManlyWadeWellmanInWeirdTales\" target=\"_blank\">Wellman in Weird Tales</a> found on the Internet Archive.<br /> <br /> *(I would have linked to the trailer of the 2024 movie version of the Crooked Man, but that story is not tied in any way to the Shonokins, and might have confused people that the story was directly relevant to this post.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-13T11:04:42-08:00","id":1547714,"title":"","content":"It\u2019s interesting to note that the Gamor would seem to function as foils to Lupus Garou - especially those of the Red Talons - in that they are (or at least begin as) wild creatures yet readily embrace Human Civilisation (or at least infiltrate it with remarkable ease) whereas the Lupus traditionally struggle with (and against) the works of Man.<br /> <br /> Out of curiosity, are the are any specifications regarding the sort of beasts the Gamor transform into? (One assumes that they would favour species that walk the fine line between wild animals that have adapted to the urban jungle and domestic species that all too often run feral, but there\u2019s no guarantee this would be the case).<br /> <br /> <br /> There\u2019s a notion ticking the far reaches of my imagination, but I haven\u2019t quite managed to put it into words: perhaps it\u2019s just the idea that the Gamor could serve as an embodiment for nature\u2019s cold pragmatism and animal indifference to the wider world - wild animals that don\u2019t really care about the wider world as a whole, much less care for the beasts that are their neighbours and sometimes their kin.<br /> <br /> Use them to highlight the way that even the most brutal Lupus care about the natural world beyond themselves and their pack, to the point of risking life and limb to fight for the Mother of Us All: the antithesis of the cold, arguably voyeuristic indifference of the Gamor.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-13T16:52:26-08:00","id":1547722,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1014508-forgotten-foes-of-the-garou-the-gamor?p=1547714#post1547714\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Out of curiosity, are the are any specifications regarding the sort of beasts the Gamor transform into? (One assumes that they would favour species that walk the fine line between wild animals that have adapted to the urban jungle and domestic species that all too often run feral, but there\u2019s no guarantee this would be the case).</div> </div> </div> </div>Their natural form is simply described as a &quot;demonic animals with claw and fang&quot;. They are also said to be &quot;semi-demonic beings, apparently bred from humans in the distant past.&quot; That is what they really look like, their true form. They can shapeshift to a <i>single</i> human form. No other description is given though there is an illustration of a bipedal humanoid type monster with claws and fangs. So different STs will come up with very different appearances.<br /> <br /> For my chronicles, I default them to an appearance somewhat similar to how the N'Garai are described/illustrated in Marvel Comics books, particularly the stories written by their creator Chris Claremont. I use elements of the N'Garai stories as part of my depiction of them (along with certain stories of Laird Barron (particularly The Siphon, collected in The Beautiful Thing That Awaits Us All), and the Leviathan storyline in Dark Shadows TV show). They are not so much animals with demonic features, but demons with some animal-like features. This, of course, has no basis in the actual creature's description which is kept fairly simple. Other interpretations are equally plausible.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1014508-forgotten-foes-of-the-garou-the-gamor?p=1547714#post1547714\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There\u2019s a notion ticking the far reaches of my imagination, but I haven\u2019t quite managed to put it into words: perhaps it\u2019s just the idea that the Gamor could serve as an embodiment for nature\u2019s cold pragmatism and animal indifference to the wider world - wild animals that don\u2019t really care about the wider world as a whole, much less care for the beasts that are their neighbours and sometimes their kin.<br /> <br /> Use them to highlight the way that even the most brutal Lupus care about the natural world beyond themselves and their pack, to the point of risking life and limb to fight for the Mother of Us All: the antithesis of the cold, arguably voyeuristic indifference of the Gamor.</div> </div> </div> </div>Given that they don't seem to be used (since their only mention is in Ways of the Wolf), I say if you think can get any mileage of it, use whatever speaks to you.<br /> <br /> I try to make them distinct from other Wyrm minions, but not so narrow that I could run only a few kinds of plots with them. If needed I want to be able to use them as recurring antagonists in one chronicle, and then use them in another chronicle and not repeat myself. That's why I tried to incorporate various influences in my portrayal. So besides the N'Garai, the beings from The Siphon, and the Leviathan cult, I've now thrown in the Shonokin and put them all in a blender.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 01-16-2025, 02:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-14T07:39:11-08:00","id":1547740,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3046_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3046\" data-vbnamecard=\"3046\">Black Fox</a>, thank you for the info: I\u2019ll have to think over this data and puzzle out my mental image of this particular monster.<br /> <br /> My gut instinct is that the individuals members of the \u2026 breed? Species? Type? \u2026 ought to be quite disparate and that their physical forms should act as something of a foil to the Black Spiral Dancers (The latter have \u2018horrifying distortion of a Garou\u2019 locked down, so it makes sense to take the Gamor in a different direction - The Wyrm as the inevitable end of all things, rather than a rampaging force of raw corruption?).<br /> <br /> Re-reading your description of the Gamor, it occurs to me that making them spies and spymasters, survivors and saboteurs par excellence, intended as much to serve as the Wyrm\u2019s<br /> eyes in the mortal world and the Wyrm\u2019s memory of all that has been done in it\u2019s name, might be the way to go.<br /> <br /> Banes, Fomori and Black Spiral Dancers come and go, but the Gamor abide eternally \u2026 at least until the ending of the World: I wonder if this gives them something of a reputation as oracles amongst servants of the Wyrm?<br /> <br /> They might well be seen as rather passive, but exhaustively well-informed and able to sneak into places other Wyrmspawn would never dare to go, by dint of being quite coldly lucid (Especially by the standards of a Black Spiral Dancer): also as enormously old, with a long memory.<br /> <br /> <br /> Here\u2019s a thought: it seems likely the Gamor would regard the Black Spiral Dancers as their own replacements in the Forces of the Wyrm, but it\u2019s interesting to wonder whether they see the BSDs as superior or inferior replacements\u2026","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-15T04:14:16-08:00","id":1547751,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1014508-forgotten-foes-of-the-garou-the-gamor?p=1547714#post1547714\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> It\u2019s interesting to note that the Gamor would seem to function as foils to Lupus Garou - especially those of the Red Talons - in that they are (or at least begin as) wild creatures yet readily embrace Human Civilisation (or at least infiltrate it with remarkable ease) whereas the Lupus traditionally struggle with (and against) the works of Man.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah honesty them serving as the dark foils of the Red Talons could open up some interesting story beat's. Like they could hold a special disdain for the tribe because they view them as Hypocrites. They rail ageist the many crimes of humanity has committed against Gaia yet the Gamor 's point of view the Talons act exactly like humans. No better than the many groups of terrorists and extremists they manipulate in serverice of the Wyrm. <br /> <br /> Now How correct the Gamor's opions on the talon depends on the ST...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25848-mrornx","name":"MrornX","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"418"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-15T12:30:36-08:00","id":1547763,"title":"","content":"Seems like a more viable enemy to use in VTM.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-15T17:45:00-08:00","id":1547769,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1014508-forgotten-foes-of-the-garou-the-gamor?p=1547623#post1547623\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm resurrecting a very old thread of mine. I think I found the possible inspiration for the Gamor. I recently started reading the <a href=\"https://www.valancourtbooks.com/john-the-balladeer-1988.html\" target=\"_blank\">Silver John stories</a> of Manly Wade Wellman.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh fun! The only one I own is The Old Gods Waken, but I remember enjoying other Silver John stories and vaguely remember a whodunnit featuring John Thunstone.<br /> <br /> Wellman's Appalachian folklore-themed stories certainly seem like they would be good fodder for WtA campaigns.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-15T23:18:02-08:00","id":1547773,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1014508-forgotten-foes-of-the-garou-the-gamor?p=1547769#post1547769\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh fun! The only one I own is The Old Gods Waken, but I remember enjoying other Silver John stories and vaguely remember a whodunnit featuring John Thunstone.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Valancourt Books reprint I linked to is fairly cheap and print on-demand through Amazon. If you're already inclined to it, I certainly recommend it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1014508-forgotten-foes-of-the-garou-the-gamor?p=1547769#post1547769\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wellman's Appalachian folklore-themed stories certainly seem like they would be good fodder for WtA campaigns.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. He mixes authentic Appalachian folklore, standard occult concepts, and his own creations quite well. Certainly a lot of things one can steal for Werewolf. The Shonokins don't appear in the Silver John stories other than the novel After Dark, but that public domain PDF I linked to has all the other stories they appear in.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-18T16:54:30-08:00","id":1547823,"title":"","content":"The bits about the parallels to the Shonokins makes me think the Gamor are masters of <i>Old</i> Sorcery. They walked the oldest mountains in existence (the Appalachians) before humanity ever existed. They mastered Banecraft, Black Geometry, and Psychetecture when mankind's antecedents struggled to turn stones and bones into tools. And now persist, folded into the human species, parasitically suckling on their suffering, while preserving what remains of their civilization's power.<br /> <br /> Not gonna lie, as a Warhammer 40K fan, I get some strong <a href=\"https://warhammer40k.fandom.com/wiki/Drukhari\" target=\"_blank\">Drukhari</a> vibes from the Gamor. Though obviously those would function more as Fomorian Changelings, than a strictly Wyrmish species.<br /> <br /> Anyway, the Gamor strike me as not merely a social or conspiratorial threat, but one that is the best informed about Arts forgotten by all but the vilest Vampire methuselah, most malicious dark Mage (probably a Lich or extremely old Nephandus), most withered Wraith, or most baleful Bane of rank. They can forge Banes into artifacts seldom dreamed, let alone wielded, and know where is hidden those artifacts they lack the knowledge to reproduce. As poor combatants at best, the Gamor prefer to parcel these artifacts or lore out to other Wyrmspawn (Fomori, BSD, etc) or to the Wyrm's mortal servants.<br /> <br /> While, naturally, keeping the best stuff for themselves.<br /> <br /> Gamor resent humanity, and the Wyrm's other servants. They consider Banes fit only as tools or slaves, of course. Their understanding of summoning, binding, warding, and using Banes is ancient and potent, if diminished. Similarly Fomori, which the Gamor learned to produce artificially when they were ascendant. Humans and Garou (or specifically the BSDs), by contrast, are their hated &quot;successors&quot;. Poorer replacements for the purity of the Gamor. Defer to the BSD, if it keeps the mongrels content and directed outward. But never kneel to them as masters, as a man would never yield to his hound. Humans, meanwhile, are cattle to be herded and fed upon, which the Gamor are often content to do passively.<br /> <br /> They've seen where mighty empires end. They saw it with their own, and they intend to survive the fall of humanity. For when the time is right, the Black Geometry threaded in the designs of city and infrastructure will usher in a return to the Gamor's prominence.<br /> <br /> Let Black Spiral Dancers, Pentex, Vampires, and Technocracy play their games. They all feed the Gamor, and all will fall. The Gamor will outlast them all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":6156,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","user":"Bluecho","date":". 01-18-2025, 07:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-18T20:28:35-08:00","id":1547825,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1014508-forgotten-foes-of-the-garou-the-gamor?p=1547823#post1547823\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The bits about the parallels to the Shonokins makes me think the Gamor are masters of <i>Old</i> Sorcery. They walked the oldest mountains in existence (the Appalachians) before humanity ever existed. They mastered Banecraft, Black Geometry, and Psychetecture when mankind's antecedents struggled to turn stones and bones into tools. And now persist, folded into the human species, parasitically suckling on their suffering, while preserving what remains of their civilization's power.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a great, evocative description. I've been making the Gamor a group of sorcerers ever since I began connecting them to N'Garai and the Leviathan cult in Dark Shadows. And reading the Shonokins as sorcerers in the John Thunstone stories just sealed it for me. TBH, I make a lot of Wyrm minions sorcerers and provide them some paths and ritual of sorcerer or infernal sorcery. To distinguish one group from another is really about limiting each group to paths and powers that fit their role in the setting and making sure other groups have their own distinctions as well.<br /> <br /> Ever since viewing The Stone Tape by Nigel Kneale on YouTube, I've built up a sorcery/technology around stones and megaliths. Another Wyrm faction uses that as well, but it still fits for the Gamor/Shonokins.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1014508-forgotten-foes-of-the-garou-the-gamor?p=1547823#post1547823\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anyway, the Gamor strike me as not merely a social or conspiratorial threat, but one that is the best informed about Arts forgotten by all but the vilest Vampire methuselah, most malicious dark Mage (probably a Lich or extremely old Nephandus), most withered Wraith, or most baleful Bane of rank. They can forge Banes into artifacts seldom dreamed, let alone wielded, and know where is hidden those artifacts they lack the knowledge to reproduce. As poor combatants at best, the Gamor prefer to parcel these artifacts or lore out to other Wyrmspawn (Fomori, BSD, etc) or to the Wyrm's mortal servants.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a good hook, but it is also one I use for the Vhujunka which in my chronicles are handled very similiar to the Serpent Men of Robert E Howard. However, the Vhunjuka are exceedingly rare. I also build on the idea that as semi-demonic beings, the Gamor are related to a unique type of extradimensional creature/demon, and that much of their magic and demons are just completely different from that of everyone else. Human wizards, occultists, historians, theologians, etc. don't have any records of the things the Gamor are involved in. Those &quot;demons&quot; don't so much want to steal men's souls or reduce the earth to fire and ash, but to colonize it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1014508-forgotten-foes-of-the-garou-the-gamor?p=1547823#post1547823\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Gamor resent humanity, and the Wyrm's other servants. They consider Banes fit only as tools or slaves, of course. Their understanding of summoning, binding, warding, and using Banes is ancient and potent, if diminished. Similarly Fomori, which the Gamor learned to produce artificially when they were ascendant. Humans and Garou (or specifically the BSDs), by contrast, are their hated &quot;successors&quot;. Poorer replacements for the purity of the Gamor. Defer to the BSD, if it keeps the mongrels content and directed outward. But never kneel to them as masters, as a man would never yield to his hound. Humans, meanwhile, are cattle to be herded and fed upon, which the Gamor are often content to do passively.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm wondering now if the Gamor could be introduced in genre appropriate ways to other game lines. Fairly easy for Mage as everything fits in that game to some degree. But how to introduce Gamor in Vampire or Changeling without players being like, &quot;what the hell is this stuff doing in my game about vampires or fairies?&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1014508-forgotten-foes-of-the-garou-the-gamor?p=1547823#post1547823\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They've seen where mighty empires end. They saw it with their own, and they intend to survive the fall of humanity. For when the time is right, the Black Geometry threaded in the designs of city and infrastructure will usher in a return to the Gamor's prominence.</div> </div> </div> </div>I really like the term &quot;Black Geometry&quot;. I'll be stealing it for my chronicles.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1014508-forgotten-foes-of-the-garou-the-gamor?p=1547823#post1547823\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let Black Spiral Dancers, Pentex, Vampires, and Technocracy play their games. They all feed the Gamor, and all will fall. The Gamor will outlast them all.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm, I like this. As my version of the serpent men, the Vhujunka are from a people that really aren't coming back. They're left with a few potent almost-immortals, and bringing back their people really isn't possible or their plan. They're just spiteful and really trying to preserve what they can of their heritage. Having the Gamor think they can bring back their dominion once mankind has fallen would set them apart. It would also explain why their building survival shelters and stockpiling resources to wait out the Apocalypse. Since the Gamor only lost power in historical (to them) memory some 10,000 years ago or so, it's not like they couldn't do so unlike the vhujunka whose empire was probably millions of years in the past.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}